Thanks for the review, and for the extra test !

Resolving it in `Form::submit()` is better: the false value is computed on the final tree, so the case I left out is fixed too, and the request handlers stop touching the payload. I tried it on top of your commit: the Form suite is green, and so are the Twig and Doctrine bridges and FrameworkBundle.

What changes for a direct `submit()`, as you said: a missing checkbox whose first false value is not `null` is now `false`, where it was a transformation failure. The same goes for a missing collection entry and for a checkbox in a missing sub-form. An explicit `null` still fails, and nothing changes with `$clearMissing = false`.

One adjustment to your snippet: I kept the `is_array()` check that `MissingDataHandler` has. Without it, a custom type whose `false_values` option is a string gets the first character of that string submitted.

With this, `MissingDataHandler` is back to what it is on 8.1 (no `$synthesiseNull` flag) and is only used to decide whether a form with an empty name is submitted. Its `allow_delete` branch and the `null` filter in `ChoiceType` (#64158) no longer affect the payload. I left them alone; tell me if you want them removed here.

Obviously, I worked on this with Claude Code. We had considered that option and left it out (the "Known limitation" paragraph): it changes direct `submit()` calls, and that looked like your call rather than mine.

